# =============================================================================
# Class Manager — every environment variable the deployed app reads.
#
# Copy to `.env.local` for local work, or paste each key into Vercel's
# Environment Variables screen for a deployment. Values here are shaped like the
# real thing and are all placeholders — nothing in this file is a secret, and
# nothing in this file works.
#
# Three groups, and the distinction between them is not cosmetic:
#
#   1. CONTRACTS §0 — the nine keys `lib/env.ts` validates with zod. ALL NINE are
#      required, and they are required at BUILD time as well as at runtime:
#      `lib/db.ts` builds its pool in a module-scope IIFE, so Next's "Collecting
#      page data" step forces the whole parse. One missing key is a failed build
#      with `env_invalid` naming the key, not a runtime 500.
#
#   2. WhatsApp Cloud — read straight from `process.env`, never through
#      `lib/env.ts`. CONTRACTS §0 fixes that object's keys and these belong to
#      the production transport rather than to the build.
#
#   3. Ops console and cron — same rule, same reason, added for the Vercel
#      deployment. All three fail CLOSED when absent.
#
# See DEPLOY.md for where each value comes from and which Vercel environments
# it belongs in.
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. CONTRACTS §0 — validated by lib/env.ts. All nine required.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# On serverless this MUST be the Supabase TRANSACTION-mode pooler — port 6543 —
# and not the direct 5432 connection or the 5432 session-mode pooler. In session
# mode every client connection holds a dedicated server connection for its whole
# life against a project-wide ceiling of `pool_size: 15`, so two warm lambdas at
# `max: 10` exhaust the pooler on arithmetic alone and every DB-backed route
# starts answering `(EMAXCONNSESSION) max clients reached in session mode`.
# Between the two pooler URLs only the port differs — same host, same credentials.
#
# postgres.js cannot send prepared statements through the transaction pooler.
# `lib/db.ts` already sets `prepare: false` for exactly this reason; do not
# remove it when you change the port, or every query fails on the second call.
#
# THE USER MATTERS AS MUCH AS THE PORT. `cm_runtime` is a LOGIN role that no
# migration creates — 0001 makes only the three nologin session roles — so it
# has to be created by hand once per database, before `npm run db:push`:
#
#     create role cm_runtime login password '<generated>';
#     grant cm_service, cm_user, cm_readonly to cm_runtime;
#
# Without it, 0006_grants.sql stops with `role "cm_runtime" does not exist`.
# Do not fall back to the dashboard's `postgres.<PROJECT_REF>` string: postgres
# is a superuser, RLS does not apply to it, and CONTRACTS §2.1 — the whole
# reason `lib/db.ts` sets a role on every transaction — quietly stops being
# enforced by anything. DEPLOY.md §1.1 has the long version.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://cm_runtime.<PROJECT_REF>:<CM_RUNTIME_PASSWORD>@aws-0-<REGION>.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres

# Required by the schema and read by nothing in `lib/` or `app/` today. They are
# still mandatory: `lib/env.ts` validates the whole object on first read, so an
# absent value throws on the first database call, not on some future Supabase
# feature. Set them and leave them.
SUPABASE_URL=https://<PROJECT_REF>.supabase.co
SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_<placeholder>

# The model client's only credential — platform.deepseek.com. Every agent turn,
# every synthesis job and every plan runs through it. Costs are in ₹.
DEEPSEEK_API_KEY=sk-<placeholder>

# The two model names, resolved at call time rather than hardcoded.
MODEL_MAIN=deepseek-chat
MODEL_SYNTH=deepseek-chat

# Link signing secret. zod enforces a 16-character minimum. Consumed by nothing
# in this build, mandatory anyway — same reason as the two Supabase keys above.
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
APP_JWT_SECRET=<32-plus-random-characters>

# The deployment's own origin, with scheme and no trailing slash. `scripts/drive.ts`
# uses it as the base for its HTTP calls and `/api/ops/config` reports it back to
# the console, so a stale value here makes a healthy deployment look misconfigured.
APP_BASE_URL=https://<your-deployment>.vercel.app

# 'emulator' keeps every send inside the simulator. 'cloud' puts real messages on
# the real number. There is no dry run between them: with 'cloud' the first tick
# that has a reminder due sends it to a live handset. A sandbox deployment should
# be 'emulator' even when it has real credentials.
TRANSPORT=emulator


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. WhatsApp Cloud API.
#
# The first four are read by `scripts/wa-cloud.ts` ONLY. The running app does not
# read them to send: §16.3 puts per-sender credentials in the `sender` row, and
# `npm run wa -- link` is what copies them there. SETTING THEM ON VERCEL IS NOT
# ENOUGH — a deployment with these four set and no `sender` row hard-fails every
# outbound at the credential gate while looking perfectly configured. DEPLOY.md
# §4.1 is the step. Re-run it after any seed — `resetWorld()` does
# `delete from sender`, which is also why seeding must be refused on a
# production deployment.
#
# The last three ARE read by the app — `app/api/webhook/route.ts`, straight from
# `process.env` — and they are the inbound half, which has no database row. On
# Vercel they must be set on the deployment even though the outbound half lives
# in Postgres, or every event Meta posts is answered with a 401.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID=<15-digit-id-from-Meta-WhatsApp-API-Setup>
WHATSAPP_WABA_ID=<15-digit-business-account-id>
WHATSAPP_SENDER_PHONE=+<country><number>

# System-user token: Business settings → System users → Generate token. Issued
# with no expiry, unlike the 24-hour dashboard token sitting next to it in the UI.
# `npm run wa -- status` prints "never expires" when that is actually true; if it
# prints a date instead, a dashboard token was pasted here by mistake.
WHATSAPP_ACCESS_TOKEN=<system-user-token>

# Meta → App settings → Basic → App secret. Two jobs, both blocking:
#   1. POST /api/webhook fails closed with 401 on every event without it.
#   2. Registering the callback URL needs an APP access token (`app_id|app_secret`).
#      Meta answers (#190) "Application Secret required" to a user token there,
#      however wide its scopes — so `npm run wa -- webhook` cannot run without it.
WHATSAPP_APP_SECRET=<app-secret>
WHATSAPP_APP_ID=<app-id>

# Any string you choose. The identical value goes in Meta → WhatsApp →
# Configuration → Verify token. Meta GETs the callback URL with it before it will
# accept the URL at all, so a mismatch shows up as "callback URL could not be
# validated" rather than as an auth error later.
WHATSAPP_VERIFY_TOKEN=<any-string-you-choose>


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Ops console and cron. Read from `process.env`, fail closed when absent.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The password for /ops/login. Holds the gate over /emulator and /api/emulator/*,
# which is full read visibility into every real parent conversation — treat it
# like a database password, because it sees everything one is worth.
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 24
OPS_SECRET=<24-plus-random-characters>

# The bearer token /api/cron/tick demands. Supabase pg_cron sends it in an
# Authorization header. Note that pg_cron stores the whole command text in
# `cron.job`, so this value ends up readable to anyone with SQL access unless it
# is kept in Supabase Vault — DEPLOY.md §3 has that variant.
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
CRON_SECRET=<32-plus-random-characters>

# '1' — the exact string, nothing else — unlocks the destructive half of the
# console: seed, reset, clock manipulation, fault injection, fabricated inbounds,
# forged delivery receipts, academy and contact deletion.
#
# LEAVE THIS UNSET ON THE PRODUCTION DEPLOYMENT. Not '0', not 'false' — unset.
# The checks are written as `=== '1'` precisely so that the variable production
# will actually have (absent) is the one that forbids the most. Set it to 1 only
# on a sandbox deployment pointed at a throwaway database, and read DEPLOY.md §5
# before you do, because seeding deletes every academy in whatever database it
# can reach along with the `sender` row holding the Cloud credentials.
# OPS_SANDBOX=1


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local tooling only — do NOT set this on Vercel.
#
# `npm run db:push` applies migrations as a role that owns the schema, which the
# runtime role deliberately is not. Migrations are applied from a workstation
# against the direct connection, never from a lambda, so the deployment has no
# use for this and setting it there only widens what a compromised build can do.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://cm_migrator.<PROJECT_REF>:<DB_PASSWORD>@aws-0-<REGION>.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres
